    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d254">

                <pb facs="0486" n="412" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg412"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Hopkins Papers</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The British Minister of Supply</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Beaverbrook</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Presidents Special Assistant</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-01-11">11 January,
                        1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Harry</hi>: You asked me to make arrangements
                    to enable the United States Government to communicate with colonies direct by
                    Cable &amp; Wireless instead of through London as at present.</p>
                <p>That project has now been carried into effect for the duration of the war
                    according to your word.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d254-f1"/></p>
                <p>I bless and praise thy matchless might,</p>
                <p>Yours ever,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Max</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d254-f1" n="1" xml:id="d254-f1-content">
                <p>In telegram No. 361 to London, January 30, 1942, not printed, Hopkins requested
                    Beaverbrook’s further assistance with regard to establishing direct
                    communications. Beaverbrook’s reply, transmitted in telegram No. 517 from
                    London, February 4, not printed, described British measures taken and to be
                    taken. (811.7441/44, 46)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
